 "literal": "Wealth having-departed they-who-forgot, they-will-say, compassion having-departed evil-deeds having-done those-who-conduct-themselves.",
 "translation": "The wise say that those who abandon compassion and live doing evil are like those who have lost their wealth and forgotten themselves.",
 "modern": "அருளை விட்டு விலகித் தீய செயல்களைச் செய்து வாழ்பவர்களை, தம் செல்வத்தை இழந்து அறிவை மறந்தவர்கள் என்று சான்றோர் கூறுவார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet compares the loss of compassion to the loss of wealth and awareness. Those who abandon compassion ('அருள்') and commit evil deeds ('அல்லவை') are described as people who have lost their wealth ('பொருள்') and fallen into forgetfulness ('பொச்சாந்தார்'). By using the same word 'நீங்கி' (having departed) for both compassion and wealth, the poet emphasizes that a life without compassion is as destitute and confused as a life ruined by the loss of all material security. Thus, doing evil is a sign of having forgotten one's true human nature.",
